// bk-agent — generated from src/bk-agent-*.js by tools/merge.js. Edit the src files, then run: node tools/merge.js
// Insurance Agent / Broker. Format: [topic, question, answer, 'B'|'D', 'E'|'M'|'S'|'X', source, { t: top, f: [follow-up q, a] }]
QB["bk-agent"] = (QB["bk-agent"] || []).concat([
["Role", "What does an insurance agent or broker do?", "**Why they ask:** the opening question.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"I help people and businesses protect against risk — assessing their needs, finding and recommending the right coverage, quoting with carriers, explaining policies clearly, closing the sale, and servicing the relationship through renewals and claims. An agent typically represents specific carriers; a broker represents the client and shops multiple carriers. The core is genuinely understanding a client's risks and getting them properly protected, then being there when something goes wrong.\"\n\nTip: Assess risk, match coverage, serve the relationship long-term.", "B", "E", null, {"t":1,"f":["Agent vs broker?","An agent represents insurers and sells their products; a broker represents the client and shops across multiple carriers."]}],
["Needs", "How do you assess a client's insurance needs?", "**Why they ask:** the foundation of good advice.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"I ask about their situation, assets, liabilities, and what they're trying to protect — then identify their real exposures, including gaps they haven't thought of. For a business, that's property, liability, workers' comp, cyber, and more; for individuals, home, auto, life, umbrella. I recommend coverage matched to actual risk, not just the cheapest or the biggest policy. Good needs analysis means they're properly protected, which is what builds trust and keeps clients.\"\n\nTip: Identify real exposures and gaps, not just sell a product.", "D", "M", null, {"t":1,"f":["What's a coverage gap clients often miss?","Underinsurance — like too little liability or no umbrella — that leaves them exposed to a loss that could ruin them."]}],
["Coverage", "How do you explain coverage to a client clearly?", "**Why they ask:** clarity prevents disputes.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"In plain language — what's covered, what's not (exclusions), the limits, the deductible, and real examples of when it would and wouldn't pay. I make sure they genuinely understand, especially the exclusions and limits, so there's no shock at claim time. A client who thinks they're covered for something they're not is a disaster for everyone. Clear, honest explanation up front prevents disputes and builds the trust that keeps clients for years.\"\n\nTip: Explain exclusions and limits clearly; no claim-time surprises.", "D", "M", null, {"f":["Why emphasize exclusions?","A client who assumes something's covered and finds out it isn't at claim time loses trust and may have a real loss — honesty up front is essential."]}],
["Sales", "How do you sell insurance ethically and effectively?", "**Why they ask:** the sales-ethics balance.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"By advising, not pushing — understanding the client's needs and recommending appropriate coverage, explaining value honestly, and never overselling or underselling to make a sale. Insurance is about trust; a client who's sold the wrong coverage or too little to win on price will leave or suffer a loss. Done right, proper coverage protects the client and builds a lasting relationship and referrals. Ethical selling and good business are the same thing here.\"\n\nTip: Advise honestly; proper coverage builds lasting relationships.", "D", "M", null, {"t":1,"f":["Why not just sell the cheapest policy to win?","Underinsuring to win on price fails the client at claim time and destroys trust — it's bad ethics and bad business."]}],
["Retention", "How do you retain clients and handle renewals?", "**Why they ask:** retention drives the business.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"I stay in touch, review their coverage at renewal to make sure it still fits as their life or business changes, proactively flag gaps or savings, and I'm responsive and genuinely helpful, especially at claim time. Clients leave when they feel like just a policy number. Reviewing needs regularly and being there when they need me — not just at sale — is what keeps them and earns referrals. Retention is far cheaper than finding new clients.\"\n\nTip: Review coverage at renewal; be there at claim time.", "B", "M", null, {"f":["When do clients most judge their agent?","At claim time — how you help when something goes wrong determines whether they stay and refer others."]}],
["Claims", "How do you support a client through a claim?", "**Why they ask:** the moment of truth.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"I guide them through the process, help them file correctly and promptly, advocate for a fair outcome with the carrier, and keep them informed and reassured during a stressful time. This is when the client finds out if the coverage I sold and the relationship are real. Being a genuine advocate at claim time — not disappearing — is what earns lifelong loyalty and referrals, and it's the right thing to do when someone's had a loss.\"\n\nTip: Advocate and guide; claim time earns lifelong loyalty.", "D", "M", null, {"f":["What if a claim is denied?","Help them understand why, check if it's a legitimate denial or worth appealing, and advocate fairly — honesty either way."]}],
["Products", "What lines of insurance are you familiar with?", "**Why they ask:** product knowledge.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"I'd describe my experience honestly — personal lines (auto, home, umbrella, life) and/or commercial lines (general liability, property, workers' comp, professional liability, cyber, commercial auto). Each has its own risks, coverage nuances and markets. Deep product knowledge lets me match clients to the right coverage and spot gaps, so I keep learning the products and carriers in my area of focus.\"\n\nTip: Be honest about your lines; show depth and willingness to learn.", "B", "M", null, {"f":["What's growing fastest in commercial insurance?","Cyber liability — as businesses digitize, it's increasingly essential and a frequent coverage gap."]}],
["Prospecting", "How do you build your book of business?", "**Why they ask:** growth is key.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"Referrals from happy clients are the best source, so great service comes first. Beyond that: networking, centers of influence (accountants, realtors, attorneys), community involvement, niche specialization, and consistent follow-up. I focus on building genuine relationships and a reputation for honest advice, which generates referrals that compound. Chasing leads matters, but a strong service reputation makes clients and partners send business to me.\"\n\nTip: Referrals from great service; networking and specialization.", "B", "M", null, {"f":["Why are referrals the best source?","They come pre-trusted, convert better, and cost nothing — they're earned by serving existing clients well."]}],
["Behavioral", "Tell me about helping a client who had a coverage gap.", "**Why they ask:** real value.\n\n**Answer (example):**\n\"Reviewing a small business client's coverage at renewal, I noticed they had no cyber coverage despite handling customer data — a serious exposure. I explained the real risk with examples, recommended appropriate coverage, and they added it. Months later they had a data incident that the policy covered. Proactively catching the gap protected their business and cemented a client for life.\"\n\nTip: Show a proactively caught gap that protected the client.", "D", "M", null, {"t":1,"f":["How did you spot the gap?","A thorough renewal review of their actual operations and exposures, not just rolling over the existing policy."]}],
["Compliance", "What compliance and ethics apply to insurance sales?", "**Why they ask:** regulated field.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"State licensing (and continuing education), suitability — recommending appropriate coverage, disclosure of terms, no misrepresentation of policies, proper handling of client funds and information, and avoiding conflicts of interest. Insurance is regulated by states to protect consumers, and misrepresenting coverage or churning policies for commission is both illegal and destroys trust. I sell honestly and keep my license and knowledge current.\"\n\nTip: Licensing, suitability, no misrepresentation, consumer protection.", "D", "M", null, {"f":["What is 'churning'?","Replacing policies unnecessarily to generate commissions, harming the client — an unethical and often illegal practice."]}],
["Closing", "Why insurance sales?", "**Why they ask:** motivation.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"I like helping people protect what matters to them, the relationship-driven nature of the work, and that doing it honestly — proper coverage, genuine service — both serves the client and builds a sustainable business. Being there for someone after a loss is genuinely meaningful, and I enjoy building long-term relationships and a book I'm proud of.\"\n\nTip: Protecting people, relationships, honest and sustainable.", "B", "E", null, {"f":["What makes a great insurance agent?","Genuine care for clients, honest advice, product knowledge, and being reliably there — especially at claim time."]}]
]);

// Insurance Agent / Broker — part 2. Format: [topic, question, answer, 'B'|'D', 'E'|'M'|'S'|'X', source, { t: top, f: [follow-up q, a] }]
QB["bk-agent"] = (QB["bk-agent"] || []).concat([
["Auto", "Walk me through the main coverages on a personal auto policy.", "**Why they ask:** auto is the most common line; they want to hear you explain it cleanly.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"There are a few distinct pieces. Liability — bodily injury and property damage — pays for harm you cause others and is what the state requires; I always push limits well above the minimum because a serious injury blows through a low limit fast. Collision pays to repair your car in a crash; comprehensive covers non-collision like theft, hail or hitting a deer. Then medical payments or PIP for your own injuries, and uninsured/underinsured motorist for when the other driver has little or no coverage. I explain each so the client isn't surprised by what a policy does and doesn't do.\"\n\nTip: Liability (BI/PD), collision, comprehensive, med-pay/PIP, and UM/UIM — and push liability limits above state minimums.", "D", "E", null, {"t":1,"f":["Why recommend above the state minimum liability?","Minimums are often low enough that one serious injury exceeds them, leaving the client personally exposed for the rest — a little more premium buys real protection."]}],
["Home", "How do you explain homeowners coverage and the difference between replacement cost and actual cash value?", "**Why they ask:** this is where clients get burned at claim time if the agent didn't explain it.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"A homeowners policy covers the dwelling, other structures, personal property, loss of use, and personal liability. The key conversation is valuation: replacement cost pays to rebuild or replace without deducting for age, while actual cash value subtracts depreciation. A ten-year-old roof on ACV might pay only a fraction of a new one; on replacement cost it pays to actually replace it, subject to the deductible. I also make sure the dwelling limit reflects rebuild cost, not market value, because you insure the cost to rebuild, not what you could sell it for.\"\n\nTip: Replacement cost ignores depreciation; ACV subtracts it — and insure the dwelling to rebuild cost, not market value.", "D", "M", null, {"t":1,"f":["Why insure to rebuild cost instead of market value?","Market value includes land and location; a total loss only requires rebuilding the structure, which can cost more or less than market — pricing to the wrong number leaves a gap or wasted premium."]}],
["Umbrella", "What is an umbrella policy and who needs one?", "**Why they ask:** umbrellas are high-value, under-sold, and a mark of a thorough agent.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"An umbrella adds liability limits — often a million or more — on top of the auto and home policies, and can be surprisingly cheap because it sits above those underlying limits. Anyone with assets or future income worth protecting should consider one: a serious at-fault auto accident or a lawsuit from an injury at home can exceed a standard liability limit, and the umbrella is what stands between the client and their savings. I frame it as protecting net worth, not as an upsell, and I check the required underlying limits are in place so there's no gap.\"\n\nTip: Umbrella stacks extra liability over home/auto cheaply — it protects assets and income from a catastrophic claim.", "D", "M", null, {"f":["Why is an umbrella relatively cheap?","It only pays after the underlying policy limits are exhausted, so large claims reaching it are rare — the insurer prices that low frequency."]}],
["Life", "Explain the difference between term, whole, and universal life.", "**Why they ask:** life products confuse clients and some agents oversell cash-value policies.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"Term covers a set period, say 20 years, pure death benefit, lowest cost — ideal for covering a mortgage or income while kids are young. Whole life is permanent with a fixed premium and a cash value that grows on a guaranteed basis; it costs much more. Universal life is permanent but flexible on premium and death benefit, with cash value tied to interest or an index. I start from the need: if it's temporary income protection, term is usually the honest answer, and I only recommend permanent coverage when there's a genuine lifelong need like estate planning or a special-needs dependent.\"\n\nTip: Term = cheap temporary protection; whole/universal = costlier permanent coverage — match the product to the actual need.", "D", "M", null, {"t":1,"f":["When is term the right recommendation over whole life?","When the need is temporary — covering income or a mortgage for a set period — buying term and investing the difference usually serves the client better than an expensive permanent policy."]}],
["Health", "How do you explain deductible, copay, coinsurance, and out-of-pocket max?", "**Why they ask:** clients routinely misunderstand their own health plans.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"I walk it in order. The deductible is what you pay before the plan starts sharing — say the first 2,000. A copay is a flat fee for a visit, like 30 at the doctor. Coinsurance is your percentage after the deductible — if it's 20 percent, the plan pays 80 percent of covered costs. The out-of-pocket maximum is the cap: once you hit it, the plan pays 100 percent for the rest of the year. So a cheap-premium, high-deductible plan can cost far more in a bad health year. I show the total potential cost, not just the premium.\"\n\nTip: Deductible first, then copays/coinsurance, capped by the out-of-pocket max — compare total exposure, not just premium.", "D", "E", null, {"f":["Why compare out-of-pocket max, not just premium?","A low-premium plan with a high deductible and OOP max can cost thousands more in a year with real claims — the worst-case total is what protects the client."]}],
["Commercial", "What is a Business Owner's Policy and what does general liability cover?", "**Why they ask:** commercial lines are higher-premium and show breadth.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"A BOP bundles property and general liability for small and mid-size businesses at a packaged price, often with business interruption built in. General liability covers third-party bodily injury and property damage — a customer slips in the store, or the business damages a client's property — plus products and completed-operations and personal/advertising injury. It does not cover professional mistakes or employee injuries; those need professional liability and workers' comp. With a business client I map what the BOP covers against their real exposures and fill the gaps separately.\"\n\nTip: BOP = property + GL (+ often BI) packaged; GL covers third-party injury/damage, not professional errors or employee injury.", "D", "M", null, {"f":["What common exposures does a BOP not cover?","Professional errors (needs E&O/professional liability), employee injuries (workers' comp), auto (commercial auto), and often cyber — all added separately."]}],
["Commercial", "What is workers' compensation and why is it different from liability?", "**Why they ask:** workers' comp is mandatory and often misunderstood by clients.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"Workers' comp covers employees injured on the job — medical costs and lost wages — regardless of fault, and in exchange employees generally give up the right to sue the employer for those injuries. It's required for most employers, with rules and rates that vary by state and by the class codes describing the work. It's not general liability, which is about harm to third parties. For a business client I make sure payroll and class codes are right, because misclassification causes audit surprises, and I explain how their claims experience drives future premium through the experience modifier.\"\n\nTip: No-fault coverage for employee injury, mandated and state-regulated; separate from third-party GL, and priced by class code and experience mod.", "D", "M", null, {"f":["What is an experience modifier?","A factor that adjusts workers' comp premium up or down based on the employer's past claims versus expected — rewarding safer-than-average loss history."]}],
["Underwriting", "What does underwriting mean, and what's your role in it as the agent?", "**Why they ask:** agents are the front end of underwriting and need to represent risk honestly.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"Underwriting is the insurer deciding whether to insure a risk and at what price. My job is to present the risk accurately and completely — the property's condition, the driving records, the business operations — so the application is clean and the policy actually responds at claim time. It's tempting to shade an application to get a better rate, but a material misrepresentation can void coverage exactly when the client needs it, which is the worst outcome. So I gather good information up front and set honest expectations about what a given risk will cost.\"\n\nTip: Represent the risk accurately — a shaded application can void the policy at claim time, which is catastrophic for the client.", "D", "M", null, {"t":1,"f":["Why is a material misrepresentation on an application so dangerous?","The insurer can rescind the policy or deny the claim, leaving the client uncovered for the very loss they bought insurance to protect against."]}],
["Advice", "When would you advise a client NOT to insure something?", "**Why they ask:** good agents give risk advice, not just sell policies.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"Insurance is for losses you can't comfortably absorb, so I advise retaining small, predictable risks rather than insuring them. For example, a very low deductible or a cheap extended warranty on a minor item often isn't worth the premium — the client is just trading dollars with friction. I'd rather they raise a deductible to lower premium and self-insure the small stuff, then use that savings toward real catastrophic protection like higher liability limits or an umbrella. Being honest about where coverage isn't worth it is what earns the trust to sell the coverage that is.\"\n\nTip: Insure what you can't absorb; retain small, predictable risks — honesty about where coverage isn't worth it builds trust.", "D", "M", null, {"f":["How does raising a deductible fit this advice?","A higher deductible lowers premium and self-insures small losses, freeing budget for the catastrophic coverage that actually protects the client."]}],
["Advice", "How do you advise a client on choosing a deductible?", "**Why they ask:** a practical, everyday client conversation.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"I frame it as how much of a loss they can comfortably pay out of pocket versus how much premium they save by taking more of it on. A higher deductible meaningfully lowers premium, but only makes sense if the client actually has that cash available without stress. I'll show the numbers — if raising the deductible from 500 to 1,000 saves, say, 150 a year, it pays for itself in a few claim-free years, but only if a 1,000 surprise wouldn't hurt. It's a budgeting decision as much as a coverage one, so I let them make it with the trade-off clear.\"\n\nTip: Match the deductible to the cash the client can absorb; show the premium savings against the extra out-of-pocket risk.", "D", "E", null, {"f":["What's the risk of too high a deductible?","If a loss happens, the client may not have the cash to cover their share, so the coverage doesn't actually help when they need it."]}],
["E&O", "What is errors and omissions exposure for an agent, and how do you manage it?", "**Why they ask:** agents can be sued for coverage gaps; this shows professionalism.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"E&O is my own liability if I fail to place coverage a client needed or misadvise them — say they assumed they had flood coverage I never bound. I manage it by documenting recommendations and especially declines in writing, so there's a record the client chose to skip a coverage I offered. I follow a consistent needs-review process, confirm requests and changes in writing, and carry E&O insurance myself. The habit that protects both the client and me is: offer the coverage, document the decision, and never leave a 'I thought I had that' gap undocumented.\"\n\nTip: Document recommendations and declines in writing, follow a consistent process, and carry E&O coverage.", "D", "S", null, {"f":["Why document coverage a client declines?","If a loss hits an uncovered area, the written record shows you offered it and the client chose not to buy — that's the difference between advice and an E&O claim."]}],
["Model", "What's the difference between a captive and an independent agent, and how does it affect clients?", "**Why they ask:** it shapes what you can offer and your pitch.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"A captive agent represents one company and sells its products; an independent agent or broker represents several carriers and shops the market. The captive advantage is deep product knowledge and sometimes exclusive offers; the independent advantage is choice — I can match the client to the carrier that fits their risk and budget, and remarket if a carrier's rates or appetite change. For the client, independence usually means more options and easier switching; the trade-off is the captive's single-carrier simplicity and loyalty programs. I'm honest about which model I'm in and what it means for them.\"\n\nTip: Captive = one carrier, deep focus; independent = many carriers, more choice and easier remarketing.", "B", "M", null, {"f":["When does the independent model most benefit a client?","When their risk is unusual or a carrier raises rates — the independent agent can move them to a better-fitting carrier instead of being stuck with one."]}],
["Ethics", "How do you handle the conflict between commission and what's best for the client?", "**Why they ask:** commissions can bias advice; integrity matters.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"I treat the client's interest as the business model, not a constraint on it. Different products pay differently, so the honest discipline is to recommend based on need and let the commission fall where it does — a cheaper policy that keeps a client for years beats a richer sale that they cancel when they realize it didn't fit. Where incentives diverge, like a high-commission permanent life policy versus term, I'm transparent about the options and costs. My book is built on referrals and retention, and both die the moment clients feel sold rather than served.\"\n\nTip: Recommend on need, be transparent where commissions differ — retention and referrals depend on it.", "D", "S", null, {"t":1,"f":["Give an example where the right call pays you less.","Recommending term over whole life when the need is temporary — lower commission, but the client is better served and stays, driving referrals."]}],
["Account rounding", "What is account rounding or cross-selling, and how do you do it well?", "**Why they ask:** multi-policy clients retain better and are more profitable.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"Account rounding means covering more of a client's needs — adding home to their auto, or an umbrella, or life — so they're fully protected and stickier. Done well it's a coverage review, not a sales push: I look at their whole picture, spot genuine gaps, and show how bundling can also lower overall cost. A client with auto, home and an umbrella in one place is both better protected and far less likely to leave over a single rate change. Done badly it's cramming products they don't need, which erodes trust, so I only round where there's a real need.\"\n\nTip: Round the account by filling genuine gaps — it protects the client better and improves retention; never cram unneeded products.", "D", "M", null, {"f":["Why do multi-policy clients retain better?","More policies in one place mean more to move and often a bundling discount, so a single rate increase is less likely to make them shop the whole account."]}],
["Renewal", "How do you review a policy at renewal to catch coverage gaps?", "**Why they ask:** proactive reviews prevent claim-time disasters.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"I treat renewal as a checkpoint, not an autopilot. I ask what's changed — a renovation, a new teen driver, a home business, a new car, a marriage — because life changes create gaps the old policy doesn't cover. I check the dwelling limit still matches rebuild cost after inflation, that liability limits still fit their growing assets, and whether new exposures like a side business or a trampoline need attention. Catching these at renewal, before a loss, is where an agent actually earns their keep versus just processing a renewal notice.\"\n\nTip: Ask what changed in their life and reprice the exposures — renovations, new drivers, home businesses, rising rebuild costs — before a loss finds the gap.", "D", "M", null, {"f":["What life changes most often create coverage gaps?","Renovations, a home-based business, new teen drivers, high-value purchases, and rising rebuild costs that leave the dwelling underinsured."]}],
["Service", "How do you handle a conversation about a rate increase the client didn't expect?", "**Why they ask:** rate increases are the top churn trigger; handling them retains clients.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"I don't dodge it. I explain honestly what drove it — whether it's a carrier-wide increase from inflation and catastrophe losses, or something specific like a recent claim or a change in their situation. Then I earn the renewal: I review their coverage to make sure they're not overpaying for things they don't need, check for discounts, and, if I'm independent, remarket to see whether another carrier fits better now. Clients forgive an increase they understand and feel you fought for them on; they leave when they feel ignored or stonewalled.\"\n\nTip: Explain the real driver, then review coverage and remarket — clients churn from feeling ignored, not from the increase alone.", "D", "M", null, {"t":1,"f":["When should you remarket a client to another carrier?","When their carrier's increase is out of line with the market or their risk profile changed — moving them keeps the relationship even if the policy changes."]}],
["Coverage", "How do you explain policy exclusions so a client isn't surprised at claim time?", "**Why they ask:** unexplained exclusions are the number-one source of claim disputes and E&O.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"I bring up the big exclusions proactively, because the worst time to learn about one is after a loss. For a homeowners policy I'll flag that flood and earth movement are typically excluded and need separate coverage, that wear and tear isn't covered, and how sub-limits on jewelry or electronics work. I'd rather have the slightly awkward conversation up front and offer the endorsement or separate policy than have the client feel deceived when a claim is denied. Clear expectations at the sale are what prevent anger — and lawsuits — at the claim.\"\n\nTip: Surface major exclusions (flood, earth movement, wear and tear, sub-limits) at the sale and offer the fix — don't let the client discover them at claim time.", "D", "M", null, {"f":["Which exclusion surprises homeowners most?","That standard homeowners policies exclude flood — it requires separate flood coverage, which many assume they already have."]}],
["Prospecting", "How do you generate referrals as a durable source of new business?", "**Why they ask:** referrals are the highest-quality, lowest-cost leads.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"Referrals come from doing the core job visibly well — being responsive, getting claims handled, and reviewing coverage proactively — then simply asking at the right moments, like after a smooth claim or a good renewal review. I make it easy by being specific: 'if you know someone buying a home, I'd be glad to help them avoid the gaps we just fixed on yours.' I also partner with people in related fields — realtors, mortgage brokers, small-business advisors — who see clients at the moment they need coverage. Referred clients trust you faster and stay longer.\"\n\nTip: Earn referrals by serving well, ask at natural moments, and build referral partners who meet clients when they need coverage.", "D", "M", null, {"f":["Who makes a good referral partner?","Realtors, mortgage brokers, and small-business advisors — they meet clients exactly when a coverage need arises, like a home purchase or a new business."]}],
["Tools", "How do you use a CRM or pipeline to manage prospects and renewals?", "**Why they ask:** organization separates agents who grow from those who leak business.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"I treat the CRM as the memory of the relationship. Every prospect has a next step and a date, so leads don't go cold; every client has their renewal, their coverage notes, and their life details so a review feels personal. I set reminders ahead of renewals to do the coverage check and flag cross-sell needs, and I track why prospects didn't buy so I can follow up when the timing changes. Without a disciplined pipeline you work only the squeaky wheels and quietly lose the clients and prospects you forgot to touch.\"\n\nTip: Use the CRM to drive next steps, renewal reviews and follow-ups — a disciplined pipeline stops business leaking away.", "B", "M", null, {"f":["What's the cost of not tracking renewals proactively?","Policies lapse or get shopped away because no one reached out first, and cross-sell opportunities pass unnoticed — avoidable churn."]}]
]);

// Insurance Agent / Broker — part 3. Format: [topic, question, answer, 'B'|'D', 'E'|'M'|'S'|'X', source, { t: top, f: [follow-up q, a] }]
QB["bk-agent"] = (QB["bk-agent"] || []).concat([
["Flood", "Why isn't flood covered by a standard homeowners policy, and how do you handle it?", "**Why they ask:** flood is the most common dangerous gap in home coverage.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"Standard homeowners policies exclude flood, so it's covered separately — often through the National Flood Insurance Program or private flood markets. I raise it even with clients who don't think they're at risk, because a large share of flood claims come from outside the highest-risk zones, and there's typically a waiting period before a new flood policy takes effect, so you can't buy it when the storm is coming. I explain their flood-zone status, what's covered versus excluded, and let them make an informed choice rather than assuming their home policy has them covered.\"\n\nTip: Flood is excluded from home policies and needs separate coverage with a waiting period — raise it proactively, even outside high-risk zones.", "D", "M", null, {"t":1,"f":["Why can't a client buy flood coverage right before a storm?","Flood policies usually have a waiting period before taking effect, specifically to stop last-minute buying — so it has to be in place well ahead of a loss."]}],
["Scenario", "A client's home is underinsured after a renovation and suffers a major loss. How would this have been prevented?", "**Why they ask:** tests whether you connect proactive service to real outcomes.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"This is exactly what a renewal review prevents. A renovation raises the rebuild cost, so a dwelling limit set years ago no longer covers rebuilding, and some policies also apply a coinsurance penalty if the home is insured below a required percentage of replacement cost — the client gets less even on a partial loss. Prevention is a conversation when the work is done: update the dwelling limit, document it, and adjust the premium. If I'd learned about the renovation at renewal, I'd have caught it; the lesson is to ask clients about changes every single year, not wait for them to volunteer it.\"\n\nTip: Renovations raise rebuild cost and can trigger coinsurance penalties — catch them by asking about changes at every renewal.", "D", "M", null, {"f":["What is a coinsurance penalty on a home policy?","If the home is insured below a required percentage of replacement cost, the insurer pays a reduced share even on a partial loss — penalizing underinsurance."]}],
["Scenario", "A client's claim is denied. How do you handle it?", "**Why they ask:** claims advocacy is where agents prove their value.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"First I find out exactly why — a genuine exclusion, a missed detail, a documentation issue, or an adjuster error — before I react. If the denial is wrong or the client has grounds, I advocate: I'll gather documentation, talk to the adjuster or claims department, and help the client file a supplement or appeal. If the denial is correct because the loss genuinely wasn't covered, I'm honest about that, explain why, and then look at whether we can close that gap going forward. Either way the client sees me working the problem on their side, which is what they'll remember and refer on.\"\n\nTip: Diagnose the real reason first; advocate hard if it's wrong, be honest if it's a true exclusion — and fix the gap going forward.", "D", "M", null, {"t":1,"f":["What if the denial is actually correct?","Explain honestly why the loss wasn't covered, then review coverage so the same gap can't bite again — honesty preserves trust better than false hope."]}],
["Life", "What is an annuity and when might it fit a client?", "**Why they ask:** annuities are complex, high-commission products where honesty matters.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"An annuity is a contract that converts a lump sum or premiums into income, often for retirement — fixed annuities pay a set rate, variable ones depend on investments, and indexed ones tie returns to a market index with a floor and a cap. They can fit a client who wants guaranteed lifetime income and has covered their liquid and protection needs first. But they often carry surrender charges and complexity, so I'm careful: they're not right for someone who needs liquidity or who'd be better served by simpler options. I explain the fees and surrender terms plainly and only recommend one when the income need is real.\"\n\nTip: Annuities convert savings to income with guarantees but carry surrender charges and complexity — fit them to a genuine income need, disclose fees.", "D", "S", null, {"f":["What's a surrender charge?","A fee for withdrawing from the annuity early, usually declining over several years — it's why annuities suit money the client won't need soon."]}],
["Life", "What is disability income insurance and why do you raise it?", "**Why they ask:** disability coverage protects income — an under-sold, high-value need.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"Disability income replaces a portion of earnings if the client can't work due to illness or injury. I raise it because for most working people their ability to earn is their biggest asset — bigger than the house — yet they insure the house and the car and leave income unprotected. I explain the key terms that actually matter: the definition of disability (own-occupation versus any-occupation), the elimination period before benefits start, and the benefit period. Matching those to the client's situation and existing group coverage is where the advice lives; the product only works if it pays when they'd actually be unable to work.\"\n\nTip: It replaces lost income — often the client's biggest asset; the definition of disability, elimination period and benefit period are what matter.", "D", "M", null, {"f":["Why does 'own-occupation' vs 'any-occupation' matter?","Own-occupation pays if you can't do your specific job; any-occupation only if you can't do any job — a big difference in when the policy actually pays."]}],
["Commercial", "How do you do a needs assessment for a new business client?", "**Why they ask:** commercial accounts require structured discovery.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"I start by understanding the operation — what they do, where, who works there, what they own, what they're contractually required to carry, and what could go catastrophically wrong. From that I map exposures to coverages: property and BI for their assets and income, general liability for third-party harm, workers' comp for employees, commercial auto if they drive, professional liability if they give advice, and cyber if they hold data. I also check contracts and leases for required limits and additional-insured obligations. The goal is a program with no surprise gaps, sized to the business's real risk and budget.\"\n\nTip: Understand the operation, map exposures to coverages, and check contract/lease requirements — build a program with no surprise gaps.", "D", "S", null, {"f":["Why review the client's contracts and leases?","They often require specific insurance limits and additional-insured status — missing those can breach the contract and leave the client exposed."]}],
["Commercial", "What is a certificate of insurance and what's the difference between an additional insured and a loss payee?", "**Why they ask:** these come up constantly in commercial servicing and are easy to get wrong.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"A certificate of insurance is proof that coverage exists, issued to a third party like a landlord or a client who requires it — but it's just evidence, it doesn't change the policy. An additional insured is actually granted coverage under the policy for their liability arising from the named insured's work — common when a contract requires it. A loss payee, by contrast, is named to receive property-loss payments because they have a financial interest, like a lender on equipment. Mixing them up causes real problems: naming someone as a loss payee when the contract required additional-insured status leaves a liability gap.\"\n\nTip: A certificate just proves coverage; additional insured grants liability coverage; loss payee receives property payouts — don't confuse the last two.", "D", "S", null, {"f":["Why does confusing the two cause problems?","If a contract requires additional-insured (liability) status and you only add a loss payee (property), the third party isn't actually covered for liability as required."]}],
["Concept", "Explain subrogation in a way a client would understand.", "**Why they ask:** subrogation confuses clients and affects their deductible.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"Subrogation is when, after your insurer pays your claim, it goes after the party who was actually at fault to recover what it paid. I explain it with the deductible angle, because that's what the client cares about: if someone else caused the loss, your insurer pays you now so you're not waiting, then pursues the other party, and if it recovers fully you typically get your deductible back. So the client doesn't have to fight the at-fault party themselves — that's the insurer's job once they've been made whole. It's one of the quiet ways coverage works in the client's favor.\"\n\nTip: The insurer pays you, then recovers from the at-fault party — and you often get your deductible back if it recovers.", "D", "M", null, {"f":["How does subrogation affect the client's deductible?","If the insurer recovers from the at-fault party, the client usually gets their deductible back, since they shouldn't bear a cost someone else caused."]}],
["Client", "How do you handle a price-shopping client who only cares about the lowest premium?", "**Why they ask:** price-only clients churn and can be mis-served.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"I meet them on price but reframe it to value. I'll find them a competitive rate, but I show what the cheap quote actually buys — the liability limits, the deductibles, whether it's replacement cost or ACV — because the cheapest policy often wins by quietly cutting coverage they'd want. If after seeing the trade-offs they still want the bare-bones option, that's their informed choice and I document it. Some price-shoppers become loyal once they realize I saved them from a gap; the ones who only ever chase a few dollars will churn regardless, so I don't distort my advice to keep them.\"\n\nTip: Compete on price but expose what the cheap quote cuts; let them choose informed and document it.", "D", "M", null, {"f":["Is it worth chasing a pure price-shopper?","Only so far — show them the coverage trade-offs honestly; if they'll churn over a few dollars regardless, don't compromise good advice to keep them."]}],
["Ethics", "What are rebating and unauthorized sales, and why do licensing rules matter?", "**Why they ask:** insurance is heavily regulated and agents must stay compliant.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"Insurance is state-regulated, and agents must be licensed for each line and state they sell in, with continuing education to keep the license. Rebating — giving a client something of value, like kicking back part of the commission, to induce a sale — is prohibited in most states because it distorts fair competition and pricing. The broader point is that I can only sell what I'm licensed and appointed for, must represent products accurately, and follow the rules on disclosures and suitability. The rules protect consumers, and a violation can cost me my license, so compliance isn't optional — it's the foundation of being allowed to do the job.\"\n\nTip: Stay licensed and appointed per line/state, keep up CE, and don't rebate or misrepresent — the rules protect consumers and your license.", "D", "M", null, {"f":["Why is rebating prohibited?","It distorts fair competition and pricing by letting agents compete on kickbacks rather than coverage and service — most states bar it to protect the market."]}],
["Bundling", "How do you explain the value of bundling home and auto beyond the discount?", "**Why they ask:** bundling retention is core to a profitable book.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"The multi-policy discount is the headline, but I explain the deeper value. With home and auto together, coverage coordinates better — a single umbrella sits cleanly over both, and claims that touch both are handled by one carrier without finger-pointing. It's also simpler for the client: one agent, one renewal rhythm, one relationship that knows their whole picture. And honestly it makes them stickier, which I'm transparent about — the convenience and coordination are real, not just a sales hook. I still check that bundling actually beats separate policies for their situation, because occasionally it doesn't.\"\n\nTip: Bundling saves money, coordinates coverage (one umbrella, one claims point), and simplifies the relationship — but verify it actually wins for their case.", "B", "M", null, {"f":["When might bundling not be the best option?","If one carrier is uncompetitive on either the home or auto for that client's risk, separate well-fitting policies can beat a bundled discount."]}],
["Life", "How do you do a life insurance needs analysis?", "**Why they ask:** right-sizing the death benefit is the core of honest life sales.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"I size it to what the family would actually need if the insured died — not a round number or a multiple pulled from the air. A practical way is to add up final expenses, outstanding debts like the mortgage, income replacement for the years the family depends on it, and future costs like education, then subtract existing assets and coverage. For example, replacing an income for the years until kids are grown plus paying off the mortgage might land at a specific figure that's very different from a generic 'ten times salary.' Then I match term length to how long the need lasts. The analysis keeps me from under- or over-selling.\"\n\nTip: Add final expenses, debts, income replacement and future needs, subtract assets and existing coverage — size the benefit and the term to the real need.", "D", "M", null, {"t":1,"f":["Why not just use a simple income multiple?","It ignores the client's actual debts, dependents, existing assets and goals, so it routinely over- or under-insures — the needs analysis gives a defensible number."]}],
["Life", "How do you explain beneficiary designations and why they need updating?", "**Why they ask:** stale beneficiaries cause real family harm; good agents review them.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"The beneficiary designation on a life policy generally controls who gets the money, often overriding a will, so it has to be right and current. I explain primary versus contingent beneficiaries, and I make a point of reviewing it after major life events — marriage, divorce, a new child, a death — because a forgotten ex-spouse or a deceased beneficiary on an old policy is a common, painful mistake. I also flag situations where naming a minor directly or an estate can create complications, so they can get the right structure. It's a small review that prevents a large problem at the worst possible time.\"\n\nTip: The beneficiary designation usually controls the payout over a will — review it after every major life event; mind minors and estates as beneficiaries.", "D", "M", null, {"f":["Why not name a minor child directly as beneficiary?","A minor can't directly receive the proceeds, so it can require a court-appointed guardian or custodian — a trust or custodial arrangement is usually cleaner."]}],
["Behavioral", "Tell me about a time you lost a client. What did you learn?", "**Why they ask:** they want accountability and growth, not blame.\n\n**Answer (example):**\n\"I lost a long-time client to a competitor over a rate increase I handled too passively — I sent the renewal, they got a cheaper quote, and left before I'd really engaged. The lesson was that a rate increase is a conversation, not a notice. Since then I reach out ahead of any significant increase, review the coverage, check for discounts, and remarket if needed, so the client knows I'm working for them. I'd rather have the slightly uncomfortable call and keep the relationship than quietly lose someone I could have retained.\"\n\nTip: Own a real loss, name the specific lesson, and show the concrete change you made to your process.", "D", "M", null, {"f":["How do you now handle renewals differently?","I proactively reach out before significant increases, review coverage and discounts, and remarket if needed — engaging rather than letting a notice go unanswered."]}],
["Growth", "How do you keep your licenses current and grow your expertise?", "**Why they ask:** professional development signals a serious, lasting agent.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"I keep up the continuing education my licenses require, but beyond the minimum I pursue designations that genuinely deepen expertise — property-casualty designations like CPCU or CIC, or life and financial designations like CLU, depending on where I'm building my book. They make me better at the actual advice, not just credentialed, and they signal to clients and carriers that I take the craft seriously. I also stay current on product and regulatory changes, because insurance shifts constantly, and an agent working from outdated knowledge quietly mis-serves clients.\"\n\nTip: Meet CE requirements, pursue meaningful designations (CPCU, CIC, CLU) for real expertise, and stay current on products and regulation.", "B", "M", null, {"f":["Why pursue a designation beyond the required CE?","It deepens genuine expertise so the advice is better, and it signals credibility to clients and carriers — more than ticking the minimum CE box."]}],
["Closing", "What makes a client trust an insurance agent for the long term?", "**Why they ask:** retention and referrals are the whole business.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"Trust comes from the client believing I'll put their interest first even when it costs me — recommending the right coverage, being honest when something isn't worth buying, and showing up when a claim hits. Responsiveness matters enormously; people remember who answered the phone during a loss. So does proactivity — catching a gap at renewal before it becomes a denied claim. The sale is a moment, but the relationship is a decade of small, honest acts, and that's what produces the referrals and retention a durable book is built on.\"\n\nTip: Put the client first even when it costs you, be responsive at claim time, and be proactive at renewal — trust compounds into retention and referrals.", "B", "M", null, {"f":["What single behavior most builds client trust?","Being genuinely responsive and present when a claim hits — that's when clients find out whether their agent actually has their back."]}]
]);

